WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:06.640
I'm Constantinos Daskalakis, also known as Costis Daskalakis. I'm a

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.460
professor of computer science at MIT and
the Department of Electrical Engineering

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:19.020
and computer science and in the computer
science and AI laboratory.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:26.000
I love my work, it focuses on data interface between computer science

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.820
and game theory economics and
there are several reasons why I'm

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:37.670
interested in this interface. One is a
very practical reason and that's that a

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:44.660
lot of human economic activity is
happening online on computation of

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:55.820
platforms so you need some handle to
design these platforms in a way that

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:03.710
takes into account besides the
computational issues also the strategic

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:08.540
an incentive issues so that's a very
fact of reason why this interface is

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:13.520
important, especially when you know a lot of the economic activities up in your

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.810
line. But there's also a deeper
philosophical reason why this is

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:22.790
important and that relates to the
foundations of game theory in economics

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:27.920
which have to do with bounded
rationality. When you make predictions

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:34.249
about what humans in a strategic
scenario are going to be doing you

00:01:34.249 --> 00:01:38.539
should also take into account that
humans and are not infinitely rational

00:01:38.539 --> 00:01:44.869
and in particular they have
computational constraints in what

00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:52.119
they're able to think about in a finite
amount of time. So you really have to

00:01:52.119 --> 00:02:00.170
inject into your game theoretic and
economic approach to analyzing behavior

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:05.840
also the computational barriers that
humans are going to be facing when

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:11.740
they're making decisions so that's yet
another reason why this interface is

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:17.420
very interesting. Lastly there are very
interesting mathematics and very

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:22.730
interesting computational questions that
arise once you start thinking about our

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:30.040
interaction that motivates that interface even more.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:37.720
Nash equilibria is a mathematical
theory whose goal is to characterize

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:46.880
what happens when strategic
individuals interact in a game or in a

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:55.870
very general notion of what a game is
which is basically a strategic

00:02:55.870 --> 00:03:03.760
interaction. Roughly speaking, a Nash
equilibria is a strategy for every

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:11.360
individual participating in that
interaction with the property

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:16.200
that given what the other players, what
the other individuals are doing a

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:24.700
no-player unilaterally can improve
his position by updating a strategy so

00:03:24.709 --> 00:03:29.300
that's a Nash equilibria. It's a choice of
strategy for every participant so that

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:36.200
everybody is happy at least they can all
in the sense that they cannot unilaterally

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.960
improve their situation by
changing their strategy. It could be that

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:47.680
you know where some global mood can make
everybody happier but every individual

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:53.300
move cannot.
So what my work with Goldberg and

00:03:53.300 --> 00:04:00.400
Papadimitriou has shown is that when
game is very complex when a strategic

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.880
situation becomes very complex it has a
lot of moving parts

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:12.780
a lot of notes for the players to attune
to update their strategies, then it may

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:19.200
be computationally really hard for the
players to actually arrive at Nash

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:28.110
equilibrium. Which philosophically implies that if agents are computationally

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:33.720
bounded which they are they will
actually not be able to discover a Nash

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:39.540
equilibrium in particular in this case
the predictions of Nash equilibrium

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:45.210
should not be trusted as you know
characterizing what really happens in

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.160
the situation of conflict.

